  },
  "segment_count": 93,
  "segments": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "start": 6.75,
      "end": 7.53,
      "duration": 0.78,
      "text": "PROFESSOR: Hi everyone."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "start": 7.53,
      "end": 10.28,
      "duration": 2.75,
      "text": "Welcome to the biochemistry help sessions."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "start": 10.28,
      "end": 14.71,
      "duration": 4.43,
      "text": "This is the covalent bonds, chirality, electronegativity,"
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "start": 14.71,
      "end": 16.74,
      "duration": 2.03,
      "text": "and hydrogen bonds module."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "start": 16.74,
      "end": 19.57,
      "duration": 2.83,
      "text": "In this help session, I'll be going over the first practice"
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "start": 19.57,
      "end": 21.27,
      "duration": 1.7,
      "text": "problem in this module."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "start": 21.27,
      "end": 23.9,
      "duration": 2.63,
      "text": "If you have not had a chance to look over this problem"
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "start": 23.9,
      "end": 28.47,
      "duration": 4.57,
      "text": "yourself, please go back, look over the problem, try it, and"
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "start": 28.47,
      "end": 30.055,
      "duration": 1.585,
      "text": "then come back and we'll go through it together."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "start": 39.48,
      "end": 42.36,
      "duration": 2.88,
      "text": "Now that you've had a chance to look at the problem, let's"
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "start": 42.36,
      "end": 43.77,
      "duration": 1.41,
      "text": "get started."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "start": 43.77,
      "end": 48.33,
      "duration": 4.56,
      "text": "First of all, what is this molecule?"
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "start": 48.33,
      "end": 51.28,
      "duration": 2.95,
      "text": "To me, this molecule looks like it's a sugar."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "start": 51.28,
      "end": 55.34,
      "duration": 4.06,
      "text": "It's a polysaccharide, to be exact, and it's actually a"
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "start": 55.34,
      "end": 58.33,
      "duration": 2.99,
      "text": "chain of glucose molecules."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "start": 58.33,
      "end": 62.02,
      "duration": 3.69,
      "text": "So in this problem, the first part of the problem asks us to"
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "start": 62.02,
      "end": 65.6,
      "duration": 3.58,
      "text": "name each circled bond in this molecule."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "start": 65.6,
      "end": 70.04,
      "duration": 4.44,
      "text": "So the first circled bond is right here, and this is a"
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "start": 70.04,
      "end": 73.32,
      "duration": 3.28,
      "text": "carbon to carbon bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "start": 73.32,
      "end": 76.31,
      "duration": 2.99,
      "text": "Now, the first thing that we can notice about this bond is"
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "start": 76.31,
      "end": 81.05,
      "duration": 4.74,
      "text": "that it's a carbon to carbon bond, and we know that carbon"
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "start": 81.05,
      "end": 84.99,
      "duration": 3.94,
      "text": "only forms covalent bonds in biological systems."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "start": 84.99,
      "end": 88.43,
      "duration": 3.44,
      "text": "So the first thing we can say is that this bond is a"
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "start": 88.43,
      "end": 89.68,
      "duration": 1.25,
      "text": "covalent bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "start": 95.01,
      "end": 98.3,
      "duration": 3.29,
      "text": "Second, we need to decide if this bond is"
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "start": 98.3,
      "end": 100.7,
      "duration": 2.4,
      "text": "polar or non polar."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "start": 100.7,
      "end": 103.24,
      "duration": 2.54,
      "text": "And the way we decide that is we look at the"
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "start": 103.24,
      "end": 106.56,
      "duration": 3.32,
      "text": "electronegativity of the atoms involved."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "start": 106.56,
      "end": 109.33,
      "duration": 2.77,
      "text": "In this bond we have two carbons."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "start": 109.33,
      "end": 111.86,
      "duration": 2.53,
      "text": "And so since they're the same atoms, we know that they have"
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "start": 111.86,
      "end": 114.04,
      "duration": 2.18,
      "text": "the same electronegativity."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "start": 114.04,
      "end": 117.9,
      "duration": 3.86,
      "text": "And bonds between two atoms of the same electronegativity are"
    },
    {
      "index": 32,
      "start": 117.9,
      "end": 119.185,
      "duration": 1.285,
      "text": "non polar bonds."
    },
    {
      "index": 33,
      "start": 124.5,
      "end": 127.97,
      "duration": 3.47,
      "text": "So we know that this carbon to carbon bond is"
    },
    {
      "index": 34,
      "start": 127.97,
      "end": 130.6,
      "duration": 2.63,
      "text": "covalent and non polar."
    },
    {
      "index": 35,
      "start": 130.6,
      "end": 133.8,
      "duration": 3.2,
      "text": "Now we'll look at the second bond circled."
    },
    {
      "index": 36,
      "start": 133.8,
      "end": 138.13,
      "duration": 4.33,
      "text": "This bond is a carbon to oxygen bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 37,
      "start": 138.13,
      "end": 140.95,
      "duration": 2.82,
      "text": "It is similar to the first bond in that it involves"
    },
    {
      "index": 38,
      "start": 140.95,
      "end": 143.9,
      "duration": 2.95,
      "text": "carbon, so we can say that it is a covalent bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 39,
      "start": 149.29,
      "end": 152.49,
      "duration": 3.2,
      "text": "What's different about this bond, however, is that it is a"
    },
    {
      "index": 40,
      "start": 152.49,
      "end": 155.34,
      "duration": 2.85,
      "text": "carbon bound to an oxygen."
    },
    {
      "index": 41,
      "start": 155.34,
      "end": 158.03,
      "duration": 2.69,
      "text": "And when you look at the electronegativities of carbon"
    },
    {
      "index": 42,
      "start": 158.03,
      "end": 162.0,
      "duration": 3.97,
      "text": "and oxygen, you find that oxygen is much more"
    },
    {
      "index": 43,
      "start": 162.0,
      "end": 164.52,
      "duration": 2.52,
      "text": "electronegative than carbon."
    },
    {
      "index": 44,
      "start": 164.52,
      "end": 170.02,
      "duration": 5.5,
      "text": "So in a carbon to oxygen bond, you actually have oxygen is"
    },
    {
      "index": 45,
      "start": 170.02,
      "end": 173.79,
      "duration": 3.77,
      "text": "greedy, and it's going to take the electrons closer to itself"
    },
    {
      "index": 46,
      "start": 173.79,
      "end": 176.85,
      "duration": 3.06,
      "text": "and not share them equally with the carbon."
    },
    {
      "index": 47,
      "start": 176.85,
      "end": 180.65,
      "duration": 3.8,
      "text": "So in this kind of bond, oxygen will be slightly"
    },
    {
      "index": 48,
      "start": 180.65,
      "end": 186.0,
      "duration": 5.35,
      "text": "negative and carbon will be slightly positive, because the"
    },
    {
      "index": 49,
      "start": 186.0,
      "end": 190.9,
      "duration": 4.9,
      "text": "electrons are attracted closer to the oxygen."
    },
    {
      "index": 50,
      "start": 190.9,
      "end": 194.15,
      "duration": 3.25,
      "text": "In a bond where you have a slightly negative side and a"
    },
    {
      "index": 51,
      "start": 194.15,
      "end": 197.525,
      "duration": 3.375,
      "text": "slightly positive side, we call this a polar bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 52,
      "start": 201.31,
      "end": 206.33,
      "duration": 5.02,
      "text": "So we can say that a carbon to oxygen bond is a polar bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 53,
      "start": 206.33,
      "end": 210.95,
      "duration": 4.62,
      "text": "So in this first part, we learned that the difference"
    },
    {
      "index": 54,
      "start": 210.95,
      "end": 214.12,
      "duration": 3.17,
      "text": "between a covalent and a non polar and a"
    },
    {
      "index": 55,
      "start": 214.12,
      "end": 217.0,
      "duration": 2.88,
      "text": "covalent polar bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 56,
      "start": 217.0,
      "end": 220.59,
      "duration": 3.59,
      "text": "The second part of the problem, part B, asks if this"
    },
    {
      "index": 57,
      "start": 220.59,
      "end": 224.28,
      "duration": 3.69,
      "text": "sugar molecule is soluble in water."
    },
    {
      "index": 58,
      "start": 224.28,
      "end": 228.05,
      "duration": 3.77,
      "text": "So first you need to know whether this molecule is polar"
    },
    {
      "index": 59,
      "start": 228.05,
      "end": 229.67,
      "duration": 1.62,
      "text": "or non polar."
    },
    {
      "index": 60,
      "start": 229.67,
      "end": 233.45,
      "duration": 3.78,
      "text": "And since we know that it has carbon to oxygen bonds, we"
    },
    {
      "index": 61,
      "start": 233.45,
      "end": 237.98,
      "duration": 4.53,
      "text": "know that it has polar bonds, so it is a polar molecule."
    },
    {
      "index": 62,
      "start": 237.98,
      "end": 239.69,
      "duration": 1.71,
      "text": "And what do we know about water?"
    },
    {
      "index": 63,
      "start": 239.69,
      "end": 243.17,
      "duration": 3.48,
      "text": "We know water is an oxygen bound to two hydrogens."
    },
    {
      "index": 64,
      "start": 243.17,
      "end": 246.96,
      "duration": 3.79,
      "text": "And since it has this very electronegative oxygen, we"
    },
    {
      "index": 65,
      "start": 246.96,
      "end": 250.62,
      "duration": 3.66,
      "text": "know that it is also a polar molecule."
    },
    {
      "index": 66,
      "start": 250.62,
      "end": 254.52,
      "duration": 3.9,
      "text": "And an easy rule of thumb is that like dissolves like."
    },
    {
      "index": 67,
      "start": 254.52,
      "end": 258.19,
      "duration": 3.67,
      "text": "So since these are both polar molecules, sugar dissolves"
    },
    {
      "index": 68,
      "start": 258.19,
      "end": 260.089,
      "duration": 1.899,
      "text": "very easily in water."
    },
    {
      "index": 69,
      "start": 260.089,
      "end": 264.15,
      "duration": 4.061,
      "text": "If you had a fat, on the other hand, which is a non polar"
    },
    {
      "index": 70,
      "start": 264.15,
      "end": 266.3,
      "duration": 2.15,
      "text": "molecule, it would not dissolve in water."
    },
    {
      "index": 71,
      "start": 266.3,
      "end": 269.03,
      "duration": 2.73,
      "text": "That's why when you put oil and water, you"
    },
    {
      "index": 72,
      "start": 269.03,
      "end": 272.43,
      "duration": 3.4,
      "text": "don't see it dissolve."
    },
    {
      "index": 73,
      "start": 272.43,
      "end": 277.71,
      "duration": 5.28,
      "text": "So now that we understand why sugar dissolves very easily in"
    },
    {
      "index": 74,
      "start": 277.71,
      "end": 280.87,
      "duration": 3.16,
      "text": "water, we'll move into the third part of the problem,"
    },
    {
      "index": 75,
      "start": 280.87,
      "end": 285.45,
      "duration": 4.58,
      "text": "where they ask what types of bonds can form between these"
    },
    {
      "index": 76,
      "start": 285.45,
      "end": 287.46,
      "duration": 2.01,
      "text": "long polymers."
    },
    {
      "index": 77,
      "start": 287.46,
      "end": 293.47,
      "duration": 6.01,
      "text": "So looking at these polymers, the first thing that we can"
    },
    {
      "index": 78,
      "start": 293.47,
      "end": 300.13,
      "duration": 6.66,
      "text": "notice is that there are these oxygens and hydrogens on each"
    },
    {
      "index": 79,
      "start": 300.13,
      "end": 302.25,
      "duration": 2.12,
      "text": "of the polymers."
    },
    {
      "index": 80,
      "start": 302.25,
      "end": 306.78,
      "duration": 4.53,
      "text": "And looking at these, we can see how you can have hydrogen"
    },
    {
      "index": 81,
      "start": 306.78,
      "end": 310.26,
      "duration": 3.48,
      "text": "bonding, which we learned about in class, between the"
    },
    {
      "index": 82,
      "start": 310.26,
      "end": 314.94,
      "duration": 4.68,
      "text": "oxygen of one sugar molecule to the hydrogen of another"
    },
    {
      "index": 83,
      "start": 314.94,
      "end": 319.92,
      "duration": 4.98,
      "text": "sugar molecule, thus forming hydrogen bonding networks"
    },
    {
      "index": 84,
      "start": 319.92,
      "end": 324.08,
      "duration": 4.16,
      "text": "between the sugar molecules."
    },
    {
      "index": 85,
      "start": 324.08,
      "end": 327.96,
      "duration": 3.88,
      "text": "And in this way, they can interact with each other."
    },
    {
      "index": 86,
      "start": 327.96,
      "end": 332.03,
      "duration": 4.07,
      "text": "So in this help session we have covered the difference"
    },
    {
      "index": 87,
      "start": 332.03,
      "end": 336.4,
      "duration": 4.37,
      "text": "between a covalent non polar bond and a"
    },
    {
      "index": 88,
      "start": 336.4,
      "end": 337.77,
      "duration": 1.37,
      "text": "covalent polar bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 89,
      "start": 337.77,
      "end": 344.28,
      "duration": 6.51,
      "text": "We have covered why water can dissolve sugar very easily,"
    },
    {
      "index": 90,
      "start": 344.28,
      "end": 347.22,
      "duration": 2.94,
      "text": "and what kind of bonds can form between"
    },
    {
      "index": 91,
      "start": 347.22,
      "end": 349.17,
      "duration": 1.95,
      "text": "these two sugar molecules."
    },
    {
      "index": 92,
      "start": 349.17,
      "end": 350.42,
      "duration": 1.25,
      "text": "Thank you."
    }
  ],
